config MCUHOME_ENTROPY_IPC
	bool "Entropy from a peer core over IPC (CTR-DRBG backed)"
	default y if DT_HAS_MCUHOME_ENTROPY_IPC_ENABLED
	depends on ENTROPY_GENERATOR
	depends on IPC_SERVICE
	# `depends on`, not `select`: MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C selects
	# CSPRNG_NEEDED, which selects ENTROPY_GENERATOR, which this symbol
	# depends on — selecting it here closes that ring and Kconfig refuses
	# the whole tree with a recursive-dependency error, in every build in
	# the workspace, not just ones that use this driver. Applications
	# enable PSA themselves (Matter builds always do); a devicetree node
	# without it is caught at configure time in drivers/CMakeLists.txt.
	depends on MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C
	select ENTROPY_HAS_DRIVER
	select MBEDTLS_CTR_DRBG_C
	# CTR-DRBG uses the low-level AES module when MBEDTLS_AES_C is
	# defined and routes through the PSA API when it is not. The PSA
	# route is unusable here: this driver *is* the PSA random source, so
	# calling back into PSA to produce randomness would invert the
	# dependency. MBEDTLS_AES_C follows from PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_AES inside
	# a PSA build (crypto_adjust_config_enable_builtins.h, reached only
	# under MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C — hence the dependency above), and
	# PSA_WANT_* symbols are promptless in this configuration, so
	# `select` is the only way to force them: plain .conf assignments are
	# silently dropped.
	select PSA_WANT_KEY_TYPE_AES
	help
	  Zephyr entropy driver for cores that have no entropy peripheral
	  but share the SoC with a core that does — the nRF5340 application
	  core, whose RNG lives on the network core.

	  Seeds a CTR-DRBG from the peer over an ipc_service endpoint and
	  serves all callers from it, so the IPC channel is used once per
	  boot and once per reseed interval rather than per request. The
	  peer must run the matching service (samples/netcore-radio).

	  Fails loud: if no seed arrives, entropy_get_entropy() returns
	  -EIO. There is deliberately no fallback to a non-cryptographic
	  generator.

if MCUHOME_ENTROPY_IPC

config MCUHOME_ENTROPY_RESEED_INTERVAL_S
	int "Seconds between CTR-DRBG reseeds"
	default 300
	range 0 86400
	help
	  Wall-clock interval after which fresh entropy is fetched from the
	  peer and folded into the DRBG. The request is issued
	  asynchronously and consumed by a later call, so a reseed never
	  makes a caller wait.

	  This driver owns the reseed policy completely: mbedTLS' own
	  request counter is disabled, because it would call the entropy
	  callback at an unpredictable moment with no peer response staged
	  and turn a routine psa_generate_random() into a hard failure.

	  0 disables periodic reseeding — the DRBG then runs for the
	  lifetime of the boot on its initial seed, which SP 800-90A still
	  considers secure, but which loses recovery from a state
	  compromise. Only sensible where the peer is known to be
	  unavailable after startup.

config MCUHOME_ENTROPY_SEED_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Seconds a caller waits for the first seed"
	default 10
	range 1 300
	help
	  How long entropy_get_entropy() blocks before giving up on the peer
	  and returning -EIO. Sized for the worst case where the peer core
	  is still booting when the first Matter operation needs randomness;
	  the request is retried several times inside this window.

	  Only the first call can ever wait this long: once the DRBG is
	  seeded every call is served locally.

config MCUHOME_ENTROPY_IPC_CONNECT_PRIORITY
	int "POST_KERNEL init priority of the IPC connect phase"
	default 90
	help
	  The driver initializes in two phases. The device itself comes up
	  at POST_KERNEL / CONFIG_ENTROPY_INIT_PRIORITY (state only, no IPC
	  traffic), and a SYS_INIT at POST_KERNEL with this priority opens
	  the IPC instance, registers the endpoint and fires the first seed
	  request.

	  Must be higher than CONFIG_IPC_SERVICE_REG_BACKEND_PRIORITY (the
	  backend device does not exist before that), which a BUILD_ASSERT
	  enforces. The default is also above
	  CONFIG_IEEE802154_NRF5_INIT_PRIO (80), so on nRF53 the 802.15.4
	  spinel host has already opened the shared instance — including the
	  network-core release and SPU setup it does first — and this driver
	  only adds an endpoint to it.

endif # MCUHOME_ENTROPY_IPC
